WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
Hey, welcome to Fascinating Frontiers, episode 56.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.200
It's April 22nd, 2026.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.520
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.620
NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.180
has completed final assembly

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.980
and is on track for September launch.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.540
SpaceX has taken an option to acquire

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.620
the AI coding company cursor for $60 billion

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.660
ahead of cursor's planned initial public offering.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.700
This deal really stands out because it shows

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.100
just how seriously SpaceX is betting

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.300
on artificial intelligence to transform

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.340
how future spacecraft get designed.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.840
The technology is expected to reshape everything

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.420
from detailed simulations of rocket performance

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.280
to the autonomous flight systems

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:42.840
that will operate with minimal human input.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.700
It is a clear signal of the deepening convergence

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.520
between the latest AI tools

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.180
and the hardware that will explore beyond Earth.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.000
I find it fascinating how software decisions made today

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.100
could fundamentally change the spacecraft we fly tomorrow

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.760
while one company bets big on the software

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.520
that will design tomorrow's rockets.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.320
Another just took a painful financial hit

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.940
on the hardware flying today.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.980
Northrop Grumman recorded a $71 million charge

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.660
in its first quarter results after an anomaly

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.760
with one of its solid rocket boosters.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.020
The problem has grounded United Launch Alliance's

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:19.580
Vulcan rocket for the time being.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.760
That means several upcoming national security launches

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.020
and important science missions are now delayed.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.440
Teams are focused on clearing the entire booster family

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.240
so flights can safely resume.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.760
These kinds of anomalies remind us

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.640
how incredibly complex these machines remain

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.520
even after decades of development.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:41.020
Setbacks like that remind us how complex these machines are

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.820
but other parts of NASA are hitting

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.880
major milestones right on schedule.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.740
NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.520
has finished integration and testing

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.420
and is scheduled to launch in September.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.260
Once in orbit it will conduct

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.680
wide field infrared surveys that can map billions of galaxies.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.660
The telescope will also hunt for exoplanets

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.480
using the technique of micro-lensing.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.900
What makes Roman special is its 2.4 meter mirror

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.380
the same size as Hubble's

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.740
but paired with brand new wide field detectors.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.460
Those detectors let it image 100 times more sky

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:14.760
in a single shot compared to its predecessors.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.540
Engineers still have to carefully fold those delicate instruments

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.540
into the launch fairing like a final origami act

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.680
before the rocket fires.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.640
I cannot wait to see what hidden populations of worlds

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.160
or clues about dark matter this new eye will reveal

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.580
in its first year of operation.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.820
Roman is going to give us an incredible new view

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.360
of the universe and new research is already changing

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:36.280
how we think about where life might be able

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.800
to form around other stars.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.620
Low mass K-type and M-type stars produce intense

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.960
ultraviolet flares that can dramatically alter

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.640
the chemistry of nearby exoplanet atmospheres.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.200
If you shrunk our sun to the size of a grapefruit

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.320
an average red dwarf would be like a grape

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:52.720
sitting just across the street.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.540
Yet its flares can outshine the star itself

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.880
by factors of hundreds in ultraviolet light.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.300
Imagine a small rocky planet inside

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.600
that star's traditional liquid water zone.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.580
Every few days a sudden magnetic reconnection

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.700
unleashes a torrent of energetic particles

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.860
and ultraviolet photons that could strip

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.820
an atmosphere in geological instance.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:15.260
These outbursts also drive complex photochemistry

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.220
splitting water molecules and creating oxygen and ozone

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.560
even in places that would otherwise stay frozen.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.980
Researchers are now developing the idea

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.560
of a UV habitable zone that could be larger

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.440
than the traditional liquid water zone.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.680
Some models show the repeated flare energy

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.060
could push the outer edge of this zone outward

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.200
by tens of percent around these long lived stars

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.960
that burned for trillions of years.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.280
We are already watching this happen

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.500
on Proxima Centauri and Trappist One

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:42.420
with current telescopes.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.460
You know what is really fascinating about this?

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.820
The genuine mystery is whether life could evolve

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.460
fast enough to survive the sterilizing bursts

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.720
or whether it might learn to hide beneath protective ice

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.240
or rock while the surface chemistry cooks.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.820
Speaking of getting a closer look at alien worlds,

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.900
one of our rovers just pulled up to the edge

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.680
of a fresh impact crater on Mars.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.360
The curiosity rover has arrived precisely

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.440
at the rim of the fresh 10 meter

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:09.100
antifagasta crater inside gale crater.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:12.520
It has begun studying the sand fill inside the crater.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.740
At the same time, the rover is scouting

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.960
its next drill target higher up Mount Sharp.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.640
This latest drive gives scientists a rare close-up view

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.160
of very recent geologic activity on Mars.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.960
It is exciting to think how a decade-old rover

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.480
can still deliver such precise and useful data

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:29.480
about fresh features.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.580
While curiosity keeps driving across Mars,

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:34.780
NASA is busy strengthening partnerships

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:35.740
back here on Earth.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.720
NASA will host an international signing ceremony

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.320
in Jordan for the latest country

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:41.680
to join the Artemis Accords.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.100
That country is Jordan.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:45.520
The agreement expands the coalition of nations

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.900
committed to peaceful and transparent exploration

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:48.920
of the moon and beyond.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.660
It is genuinely encouraging to see more countries

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.560
formally align on these principles.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:56.920
Expanding the circle of nations working together in space

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.880
also means spreading the work across many communities.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:02.160
Companies in Tolohoma, Tennessee,

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.660
and NASA's Stennis Space Center supplied

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.260
and tested critical systems for the recent crude Orion flight.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.260
Their contributions were essential

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.640
to the Artemis II mission.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.040
This highlights how the Artemis program

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.240
distributes technical expertise and economic benefit

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:18.840
across dozens of communities.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.960
It is always nice to see how space exploration

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.820
creates real opportunities far from the launchpads.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.760
It is not just professionals pushing space forward,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.020
backyard observers and citizen scientists

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:31.800
continue to amaze us too.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.360
Amateur astrophotographers using everything

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:38.040
from iPhones to Nikon DSLRs and Sony mirrorless cameras

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.220
have shared sharp images of the moon,

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:45.260
starfields and galaxies M81, M82, and M101.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.960
These pictures demonstrate how accessible equipment

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.460
combined with careful processing

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:51.460
can still reveal real celestial beauty.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.240
It is inspiring that hobbyists can produce images

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.680
that feel almost professional.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.220
That same spirit of curiosity is also

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.140
driving some very big science projects.

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.180
University of Virginia professor Satya Goncho,

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:05.880
A Goncho is part of the DESI team,

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.040
building the largest three-dimensional map of the universe.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.980
The survey uses observations of 46 million galaxies

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:15.440
in quasars plus 19 million stars.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.180
This enormous data set aims to reveal

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.600
how dark energy has driven cosmic expansion

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:21.800
over billions of years.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:24.620
It is incredible to think about the scale of data

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.320
involved in what it might tell us

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.040
about the universe's hidden forces,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.500
from the very large scale of the universe

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:31.900
down to our own backyard.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.400
Families using a 102 millimeter refractor

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.740
enjoyed clear views of Jupiter's cloud bands,

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.200
Venus and the lunar terminator in early evening.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.820
The simple setup on a peer-mounted mount

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:44.560
shows how quickly the solar system

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.420
becomes accessible even to young viewers.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:48.360
It is wonderful to picture kids

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.640
getting their first real look at Jupiter's stripes

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.940
through a modest telescope.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:53.720
Moments like that are often what

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:55.420
spark a lifelong interest in astronomy.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.420
Before we go, keep an eye on how the Roman telescopes

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.340
upcoming launch and the DESI mapping results

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.300
might reshape our understanding of both exoplanets

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:06.460
and cosmic expansion in the coming months.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:09.220
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

